# Linux User Account & Sudo Security Lab

## Overview

This hands-on lab demonstrates basic Linux system administration and security concepts involving user accounts, authentication, authorization, sudo privileges, and root account restrictions.

The lab was completed in an Ubuntu Linux virtual machine and focuses on applying the principle of least privilege by providing administrative privileges only to an authorized user.

## Objectives

- Create and manage Linux user accounts
- Configure user passwords
- Verify Linux account information
- Install and configure sudo
- Assign administrative privileges to a specific user
- Test access to protected system files
- Restrict direct root login
- Verify user and group permissions
- Demonstrate the principle of least privilege

## Environment

- Operating System: Ubuntu Linux
- Virtual Machine: VMware
- Shell: Bash
- Administrative Tool: sudo

## Lab Tasks

### 1. Create User Accounts

Created the following accounts:

- `omega3` — administrative lab user
- sunflower — standard user

The accounts were created using the Linux `useradd` command.

Example:

```bash
useradd -c "Cocoa" -s /bin/bash -m omega3

### 2. Configure sudo

The sudo package was installed and the mlyons3 account was added to the sudo group.

apt update
apt install sudo
usermod -aG sudo omega3

The sudo group was verified with:
grep '^sudo:' /etc/group

### 3. Test Privilege Differences

The difference between a standard user and a sudo-enabled user was tested by attempting to access /etc/shadow.

A standard user was denied access:

cat /etc/shadow

The authorized sudo user was able to access the file using:

sudo cat /etc/shadow

This demonstrated the difference between normal user permissions and elevated administrative privileges.

Sensitive password hashes from /etc/shadow are not included in this repository.

### 4. Restrict Root Login

The root account's login shell was changed to /usr/sbin/nologin.

sudo /usr/sbin/usermod -s /usr/sbin/nologin root

The configuration was verified with:

grep '^root:' /etc/passwd

The resulting configuration showed:

root:x:0:0:root:/root:/usr/sbin/nologin

An attempt to start a root session using:

su - root

resulted in an authentication failure.

### 5. Verify User and Group Configuration

The final configuration was verified using:

id omega3
id sunflower
grep '^sudo:' /etc/group
grep '^root:' /etc/passwd

The results confirmed that:

mlyons3 had sudo privileges
The Mason NetID account did not have sudo privileges
The root account was configured with /usr/sbin/nologin
Security Concepts Demonstrated
Principle of Least Privilege

Administrative privileges were assigned only to the account that required them.

Authentication

User passwords were configured and authentication was tested during account login.

Authorization

Different users were given different levels of access based on their group membership.

Privilege Escalation

The sudo command was used to demonstrate controlled elevation from a standard user to administrative privileges.

Access Control

Access to /etc/shadow was tested to demonstrate Linux file permissions and administrative access.

Account Hardening

Direct interactive root login was restricted by changing the root shell to:

/usr/sbin/nologin


Evidence

Screenshots documenting the lab are included in the screenshots directory.

